      <p>Med spas sit where beauty meets medicine, and Google Ads treats them that way. Some services can be advertised freely, others are tightly restricted, and the wording of a single headline can decide whether an ad runs at all. Meanwhile, the most valuable clients are not first-time bookings but people who return for courses, maintenance and memberships.</p>

      <h2>Start with what you are allowed to say</h2>
      <p>Google restricts advertising for prescription medicines, and that affects med spas directly. Brand names of prescription injectables in ads, keywords or landing pages are a frequent cause of disapprovals, and medical weight loss involving prescription drugs faces strict limits. Rules differ by country, and Google applies them to landing pages as well as ads.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Describe treatments in plain terms rather than relying on prescription brand names</li>
        <li>Avoid claims about results that cannot be supported</li>
        <li>Keep images clinical and respectful; before-and-after images and body imagery are reviewed closely</li>
        <li>Check policies for your country before launching campaigns for any prescription-based treatment</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Getting this right first saves weeks of disapprovals and appeals later.</p>

      <h2>Campaigns by treatment</h2>
      <p>People search for a specific treatment, compare a few clinics, and book a consultation. Each main treatment deserves its own ad group and landing page:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Laser hair removal, often driven by price and package searches</li>
        <li>Skin treatments such as chemical peels, microneedling and facials</li>
        <li>Body treatments, where available and permitted</li>
        <li>Injectable treatments, worded within the rules above</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Treatment-specific pages that explain what happens, how many sessions are typical, what it costs and who performs it convert far better than a general services page.</p>

      <h2>Trust decides the booking</h2>
      <p>Aesthetic treatments are personal, and clients choose carefully. The questions behind every click are: who will treat me, are they qualified, and what do other clients say? Ads and landing pages should answer those directly with practitioner credentials, medical oversight where it applies, genuine reviews and clear consultation offers. Use only claims you can support.</p>

      <h2>A client is worth more than a first visit</h2>
      <p>Many treatments are courses, many need maintenance, and many clients add other treatments over time. If the account counts every booking as equal, it will chase cheap one-off visitors.</p>
      <p>Value conversions by treatment, give memberships and packages a higher value, and where possible feed repeat-client value back from your booking system. The <a href="/tools/metrics-calculator/">metrics calculator</a> helps work out what a new client is worth paying for.</p>

      <h2>Remarketing has limits here</h2>
      <p>Google restricts personalised advertising based on health and some treatments, which limits remarketing for many med spa services. Search does most of the work, capturing people actively looking for a treatment. Where remarketing is allowed for a service, it can help, but check the rules before building lists.</p>

      <h2>Where med spa budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Training and certification searches from people entering the industry</li>
        <li>At-home devices and products, such as home laser kits and skincare sold online</li>
        <li>Treatments you do not offer, especially surgical procedures if you are non-surgical</li>
        <li>Free consultation hunters with no intention to book, if free consultations are not your model</li>
      </ul>

